  "statement": "Congress should reject the Safetyist proposal to build a centralized, government-led AI monolith because state-run labs guarantee technological stagnation. The Safetyist argues that unmanaged commercial scaling invites catastrophe, and that only mandatory state safety checkpoints can prevent civilizational risk. That argument correctly identifies the stakes of advanced artificial intelligence, but diagnoses the wrong disease by treating dynamic market competition as the hazard. The truth is that centralized procurement agencies move at the speed of the Federal Acquisition Regulation, while open developer ecosystems iterate weekly.\n\nHistory proves that decentralized experimentation outperforms central planning every single time. Open-source ecosystems and cloud computing providers like Amazon Web Services scaled global digital infrastructure faster than any government committee ever could. When the Department of Defense launched the Joint Warfighting Cloud Capability, bureaucratic delays stalled deployment for four years while commercial providers shipped updates daily. Central planning in artificial intelligence means freezing the frontier while foreign competitors capture it.\n\nNational security depends on physical infrastructure abundance, not on compliance certificates issued by bureaucrats. The United States needs massive silicon fabrication plants, dedicated nuclear energy grids, and millions of advanced graphics processing units deployed in competitive datacenters. The CHIPS Act showed that Congress can fund physical manufacturing capacity without turning the federal government into an authoritarian gatekeeper of software code. If policymakers want to secure technological leadership, they must subsidize energy and silicon, not establish gatekeeping boards that add eighteen months of delay to every model weight update.\n\nEvery month a centralized licensing board delays advanced training runs, real people suffer from curable diseases and preventable workplace drudgery. When regulators choose to freeze development out of hypothetical fear, they choose to let cancer patients die today of diseases algorithms could automate cures for. Who gave federal bureaucrats the moral authority to make that choice for millions of suffering citizens? The path to national security and human flourishing is permissionless iteration, not top-down institutional paralysis.",
